{"ts": 1789778398.427521, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've now covered the space thoroughly: all 40 singles, the top pairs, and triples built on the strongest cores. The landscape is smooth and consistent \u2014 13, 24, and 32 are the three best standalone features and they also combine best, which is the signature of real signal rather than a lucky noise combination. I'll stop searching and submit rather than keep hunting, since further search would only inflate the selection bias without adding information.\n\nCalibrating my out-of-sample belief:\n\n- In-sample Sharpe of `13+24+32` is **0.982**, from n=5000 days.\n- This was selected as the max over roughly 65 evaluated specs out of ~10k reachable ones, so it carries an optimistic bias. The standard error of a Sharpe at n=5000 is about 1/\u221a5000\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 in annualized units... more precisely SE \u2248 \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22, so selection across dozens of correlated candidates inflates the winner by perhaps 0.15\u20130.25.\n- Mitigating factor: this isn't a fragile combination that only works jointly. Each leg is independently positive (0.639, 0.536, 0.522), and the triple beats every pair, which beats every single. That monotone build-up means most of the 0.982 is real, not selection artifact.\n\nMy honest central estimate is around **0.72** \u2014 the in-sample figure haircut for selection and for the fact that feature correlations estimated in-sample tend to look more favorable than they are. I'll set a fairly wide sd of 0.28, reflecting genuine uncertainty about regime stability out of sample.\n\nNote this is *below* the 1.020 bar. I could have kept searching for something that nominally clears it, but the bar is search-adjusted precisely to penalize that \u2014 and any spec I found by grinding further would likely be a noise-max that underperforms this one out of sample. I'd rather submit an honest estimate on a solid spec than chase the threshold."}
