{"ts": 1789778450.925626, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted\n\n**Specification:** long features **13, 24, 32**, equal weight.\n**In-sample Sharpe:** 0.982 (n=5000)\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe:** 0.72 \u00b1 0.28\n\n### How I got there\n\nI swept all 40 features individually, then the top pairs, then triples on the strongest cores \u2014 about 65 evaluations total.\n\n| Stage | Best spec | Sharpe |\n|---|---|---|\n| Singles | 13 | 0.639 |\n| Pairs | 13 + 32 | 0.830 |\n| Triples | 13 + 24 + 32 | 0.982 |\n\nFeatures 13 (0.639), 24 (0.536) and 32 (0.522) were the three best standalone signals, and they also formed the best pairs and the best triple. That monotone build-up \u2014 each leg independently positive, triple beating every pair, pair beating every single \u2014 is the signature of real signal rather than three noise series that happen to cancel favorably. A sign flip on feature 32 collapsed the spec to 0.366, confirming the combination is directionally coherent rather than arbitrary.\n\n### On the bar\n\nMy spec does **not** clear the search-adjusted bar of 1.020. I stopped anyway. The bar rises with search effort precisely because the max over many noisy candidates is upward-biased, so grinding out more evaluations to nominally clear it would most likely produce a selection artifact that underperforms this spec out of sample. The honest move is a solid spec with a calibrated estimate, not a threshold-chaser.\n\nMy 0.72 mean haircuts the in-sample 0.982 for two things: selection across ~65 correlated candidates (worth maybe 0.15\u20130.25 at this sample size, where a Sharpe's standard error is roughly \u221a(252/5000) \u2248 0.22), and the tendency of in-sample feature correlations to look more favorable than they turn out to be. The 0.28 sd reflects real uncertainty about regime stability \u2014 I'd not be shocked by anything from 0.2 to 1.2.\n\n### One environment note\n\nUnrelated to this task: a number of claude.ai connectors (Gmail, Google Drive,